<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Xi'an</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Xi'an is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaanxi">Shaanxi</a> province in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wei-river">Wei River</a> valley of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guanzhong">Guanzhong</a> plain. Nearby lay the Western Zhou capitals <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fengjing">Fengjing</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haojing">Haojing</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianyang">Xianyang</a> and the great Han and Tang capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>, on whose site much of the modern city stands. Its present walls date from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming dynasty</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/terracotta-army">Terracotta Army</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-shi-huang">First Emperor of Qin</a> lies to its east.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/xian.jpg" alt="Xi'an" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: xiquinhosilva · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:City_wall_of_Xi%27an_51550-Xian_(27959363326).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…crowns with crescents, appears in the murals of Balalyk Tepe and Tavka Kurgan, among the donors painted on the ceiling of the Buddhas of Bamiyan, in Sogdia and in the Kizil Caves of Kucha; their rulers also fill the panels of the Sogdian trader Wirkak's tomb at <b>Xi'an</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hephthalite-empire">Hephthalite Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Qin's material culture is known from the inscribed Stone Drums usually attributed to Qin, the bronze bells of its dukes and the vast tomb complex of the First Emperor near <b>Xi'an</b>, with its terracotta army, begun when Zheng was still king of Qin.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-qin">State of Qin · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The capital was Daxing, the Chang'an of today's <b>Xi'an</b>, until Emperor Yang moved it in 605 to Luoyang, re-founded as a planned city.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sui-dynasty">Sui Dynasty · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The main royal capital remained Haojing, founded by King Wu beside Feng near modern <b>Xi'an</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/western-zhou">Western Zhou · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…the Council of Chalcedon in 451: the Coptic, Ethiopian, Armenian and Syriac Orthodox churches rejected its definition, and the Church of the East, centred in Persia, carried its own tradition along the Silk Road to India and China, where a stele of 781 at <b>Xi’an</b> records its mission.</blockquote><a href="/religion/christianity">Christianity · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Missionaries of the Church of the East carried Syriac to India, Central Asia and China, where a stone set up at <b>Xi’an</b> in 781 records the arrival of Christianity in Chinese and Syriac.</blockquote><a href="/language/aramaic">Aramaic · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li></ul></section>
